        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A walking pad is a treadmill with the frame subtracted, and that subtraction is both the
            product and the limit. <strong class="text-white">It is the only home cardio machine
            most people genuinely put away</strong> — which is why it gets used, and why it cannot
            run, climb or take a session.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Check the minimum speed before anything else, put it on
            <a href="/treadmill-mat/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">a hard mat</a>,
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">lubricate it on schedule</a>,
            and attach the walking to calls and reading rather than to a daily number. Then treat it
            as replacing sitting —
            <a href="/treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">real sessions</a>
            are a separate thing, and you want both.
          </p>
